  "reading": "A power_fpc slope interval that covers -1/2 and lies below 0 means the spread shrinks as averaging over randomly drawn images predicts. A fitted floor at the level of the training noise means the spread left at large N is training noise, which no choice of images removes; a floor clearly above it (floor_above_training_noise: the lower bound of sigma_floor above the upper bound of the pooled training-noise SD) means some subset-dependent spread remains: room for selection.",
  "assumption": "At a ladder rung the seed changes the subset and the training randomness, so SD^2 ~ sampling + training noise. That the training noise does not depend on N is an assumption. The power-law fit treats the rungs as independent, but the rungs of one seed are nested subsets, so the SDs at neighbouring rungs are correlated and the slope interval is approximate, likely too narrow (synthetic nested data: about 89% coverage). The same holds for the parametric intervals of the variance model. The -1/2 reference of 'power' is that of an unlimited pool; 'power_fpc' corrects for drawing from the finite pool (fpc column), under which pure random sampling has slope -1/2 again.",
  "limit": "The SD over random subsets says how far one random draw strays from another. It does not bound what an informed selector can reach, because active selection can beat every random subset: the result answers 'does the choice of random subset matter', not 'is active learning useless'.",
